# Cursor Rules - Slack Standup Bot

## Editor Configuration

### Indentation
- Use 2 spaces for indentation, not tabs
- Configure your editor to display tab characters as 2 spaces
- Ensure consistent indentation across all files

### Line Endings
- Use LF (\n) line endings (Unix style), not CRLF
- Configure git to automatically normalize line endings
- Configure your editor to show whitespace characters

### Encoding
- Use UTF-8 encoding for all files
- Avoid using non-ASCII characters in code
- Include the BOM (Byte Order Mark) only when necessary

## Code Navigation

### File Structure
- Always scan the imports at the top of a file to understand dependencies
- Check function exports at the bottom of the file
- When editing a route handler, look for corresponding service functions

### Function Navigation
- Maintain a clear separation between route handlers and service logic
- Route handlers should be thin and delegate to service functions
- Look for exported functions to understand a module's interface

## Code Editing

### Code Style
- Follow existing code style when editing files
- Use Prettier for code formatting
- Maintain consistent function parameter ordering
- Keep line length under 100 characters

### Comments
- Use JSDoc for all exported functions
- Include @param and @return tags
- Comment complex logic but prefer readable code
- Use TODO comments for pending work, include issue reference

## Refactoring

### Function Extraction
- Extract reusable logic into helper functions
- Keep functions focused on a single responsibility
- When function exceeds 20 lines, consider refactoring
- Use meaningful names for extracted functions

### Variable Naming
- Use descriptive variable names
- Prefix boolean variables with 'is', 'has', 'should', etc.
- Use consistent variable naming across the codebase
- Avoid abbreviations except for common ones (e.g., ID, URL)

## Testing Workflow

### Test-Driven Development
- Write tests before implementing features
- Run tests frequently during development
- Fix failing tests before continuing
- Update tests when changing functionality

### Debug Configuration
- Configure debugger for Node.js
- Use breakpoints rather than console.logs
- Set up watch points for specific variables
- Configure launch configurations for different environments

## Git Integration

### Commit Workflow
- Stage changes logically, not all at once
- Review each changed file before committing
- Write meaningful commit messages
- Reference issue numbers in commit messages

### Branch Management
- Create feature branches from main
- Keep branches short-lived
- Rebase before creating pull requests
- Delete branches after merging

## Slack API Development

### Block Kit Builder
- Use Block Kit Builder to design message layouts
- Test message rendering on mobile and desktop
- Test with different themes (light/dark)
- Copy formatted JSON from Block Kit Builder for implementation

### API Testing
- Use Postman or similar tool to test Slack API endpoints
- Save API responses for reference
- Test error cases as well as success paths
- Document API behavior differences

## Standup Bot Specific

### Feature Implementation
1. Update configuration schema if needed
2. Add/modify service functions in `/services`
3. Connect to the appropriate route handler
4. Test the integration end-to-end
5. Update documentation

### Debugging Flows
- For standup initiation issues, check `standupScheduler.js`
- For user interaction issues, check `standupLogic.js`
- For Slack API issues, check `slackClient.js`
- For configuration issues, check `configStore.js`

## Error Handling

### General Patterns
- Use try/catch blocks around async operations
- Prefer the await-to-js pattern for error handling
- Always provide contextual information in error messages
- Log all errors with appropriate severity levels

### Slack API Errors
- Handle rate limiting appropriately
- Implement exponential backoff for retries
- Check token validity before API calls
- Distinguish between temporary and permanent errors

## Code Review

### Pull Request Guidelines
- Keep PRs focused on a single feature or bug fix
- Include tests for all new functionality
- Update documentation for API changes
- Ensure all CI checks pass

### Review Checklist
- Check for security issues
- Verify error handling
- Look for potential race conditions
- Ensure consistent naming and style